---
import ModuleLayout from '../../layouts/ModuleLayout.astro';
import Ben from '../../components/Ben.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import Widget from '../../components/Widget.astro';
import { accumulationCode, mixedPrecisionCode, quantizationCode } from '../../data/module4-code';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---
<ModuleLayout
  id="m4"
  heading="Pack the bag smarter: single-GPU tricks"
  lede="Your GPU memory is a backpack. When it overflows, you can buy a bigger bag (expensive), pack smarter (this module), or share the load (Module 5)."
>
  <div class="grid2" style="align-items:center">
    <figure class="card" style="display:grid;place-items:center;background:var(--stage)"><img src={url('assets/img/bag_full.webp')} alt="An open purple backpack holding books labelled Model Weights, Activations, Gradients and Optimizer States" style="max-height:300px"></figure>
    <div class="grid2" style="grid-template-columns:1fr">
      <div class="tile t-blue"><h4>Quantization</h4><p>Smaller items: paperbacks instead of hardcovers.</p></div>
      <div class="tile t-sage"><h4>Mixed precision</h4><p>Mix sizes: full folders only for important documents.</p></div>
      <div class="tile t-lav"><h4>Gradient accumulation</h4><p>Several smaller trips instead of one overloaded one.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ================= QUANTIZATION ================= -->
  <h2>Quantization: keep fewer digits</h2>
  <figure class="card" style="background:var(--stage)"><img src={url('assets/img/books.webp')} alt="Four large hardcover books become four small paperbacks" style="margin:auto;max-height:150px"></figure>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>Why?</b> To make a model smaller, so it needs less memory and answers faster. We already do this with π: 3.14159265 becomes 3.14. Quantization stores each number with fewer bits, at a small cost in accuracy.</p>

  <h3>How it works</h3>
  <p>Pick a small set of evenly spaced levels, then snap every number to the nearest one. The scale <i>s</i> sets the gap between levels. The zero-point <i>z</i> shifts the levels when the numbers aren't centred on zero.</p>
  <figure class="eq" aria-label="q equals round of x divided by s, plus z">
    <math display="block"><mrow><mi class="ct">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>round</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi class="cg">x</mi><mi class="co">s</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="cv">z</mi></mrow></math>
    <figcaption class="legend"><span><i class="bg-g"></i><b>x</b> original number</span><span><i class="bg-t"></i><b>q</b> stored integer</span><span><i class="bg-o"></i><b>s</b> scale</span><span><i class="bg-v"></i><b>z</b> zero-point</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>To use a number, the GPU turns it back: <i>x</i> ≈ <i>s</i> × (<i>q</i> − <i>z</i>). The small gap left over is the quantization error. For int8 with <i>z</i> = 0, the scale is the largest |<i>x</i>| ÷ 127, so the biggest number lands exactly on 127.</p>
  <Widget title="Quantize eight numbers" tag="Interactive" id="w-qz">
    <div class="ctl">
      <div><label>Bits</label><div class="seg" id="qzBits"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="8">8-bit</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="4">4-bit</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="2">2-bit</button></div></div>
      <div><label>Scales</label><div class="seg" id="qzScale"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="8">One for all</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="4">One per group of 4</button></div></div>
      <div><label for="qzOut">Outlier</label><label style="font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)"><input type="checkbox" id="qzOut"> Make one number much bigger</label></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="data quant" id="qzTable"></table></div>
    <div class="readout" style="margin-top:14px"><div><small>Scale s</small><strong id="qzS"></strong></div><div><small>Integers allowed</small><strong id="qzR"></strong></div><div><small>Average error</small><strong id="qzE"></strong></div></div>
    <div class="verdict" id="qzV"></div>
  </Widget>

  <h3>Benefits and trade-offs</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="tile t-sage"><h4>Benefits</h4><ul>
      <li>4-bit weights need about a quarter of the memory of 16-bit ones.</li>
      <li>Faster text generation: each new token reads fewer bytes from memory (Module 6).</li>
      <li>Bigger models fit on smaller, cheaper GPUs.</li>
    </ul></div>
    <div class="tile t-peach"><h4>Trade-offs</h4><ul>
      <li>Some quality loss: 8-bit is close to lossless, 4-bit usually loses a little, 3-bit and 2-bit lose a lot.</li>
      <li>Small models suffer more than big ones.</li>
      <li>Not always faster: it depends on the GPU, the software and the batch size.</li>
      <li>You can't train quantized weights directly. QLoRA freezes a 4-bit model and trains small 16-bit adapters instead.</li>
    </ul></div>
  </div>
  <Widget title="Same model, four formats" tag="Interactive" id="w-quant">
    <div class="ctl"><div><label for="qN">Parameters <output id="qNo"></output></label><input type="range" id="qN" min="0" max="100" value="45"></div><div><label for="qV">GPU memory</label><select id="qV"><option value="16" selected>16 GB</option><option value="24">24 GB</option><option value="40">40 GB</option><option value="80">80 GB</option></select></div></div>
    <div id="qBars" style="display:grid;gap:10px;margin-top:6px"></div>
  </Widget>

  <h3>In practice</h3>
  <CodeTabs title="Run it yourself" tabs={quantizationCode} />
  <p><b>Libraries:</b> <code>bitsandbytes</code> with Hugging Face Transformers (load any model in 8 or 4 bits), Unsloth (fine-tune in 4 bits with QLoRA), <code>torchao</code> (PyTorch's own quantization), llm-compressor and GPTQModel (make GPTQ or AWQ checkpoints), and vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, llama.cpp and Ollama (serve quantized models).</p>
  <div class="take">Quantization trades a little accuracy for a lot of memory. It shines when serving models. For training, use mixed precision, or QLoRA to fine-tune.</div>

  <!-- ================= MIXED PRECISION ================= -->
  <h2>Mixed precision: fast where we can, precise where we must</h2>
  <figure class="card" style="background:var(--stage)"><img src={url('assets/img/folders.webp')} alt="Four big folders become two big folders for important documents and two compact ones" style="margin:auto;max-height:150px"></figure>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>Why?</b> Training in float32 is slow and memory-hungry. GPUs do 16-bit maths many times faster on their Tensor Cores, and 16-bit activations take half the memory. But a few steps really need float32's precision.</p>

  <h3>How it works</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><span>Keep the weights in <b>float32</b>. This is the master copy.</span></li>
    <li><span>Run the forward and backward passes in mixed precision: matrix multiplications in <b>bfloat16</b>, and sensitive steps like softmax, normalization and the loss in float32. PyTorch's <code>autocast</code> picks for you.</span></li>
    <li><span>The gradients arrive in float32, and the optimizer updates the float32 weights, so tiny updates aren't rounded away.</span></li>
    <li><span>With <b>float16</b> only: multiply the loss by a big number before the backward pass, so tiny gradients don't round to zero, then divide them back before the update. This is <b>loss scaling</b>. bfloat16 has float32's range, so it doesn't need it.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p>Why must the update stay in float32? Try it:</p>
  <Widget title="Can a tiny update survive?" tag="Real bfloat16 rounding" id="w-mp">
    <div class="ctl"><div><label for="mpW">Weight</label><input type="number" id="mpW" value="1" step="0.1"></div><div><label for="mpU">Update (learning rate × gradient)</label><input type="number" id="mpU" value="0.001" step="0.0001"></div></div>
    <div class="readout"><div><small>float32 result</small><strong id="mp32"></strong></div><div><small>bfloat16 result</small><strong id="mp16"></strong></div></div>
    <div class="verdict" id="mpV"></div>
  </Widget>
  <figure class="eq" aria-label="Weight update: w becomes w minus eta times gradient of L with respect to w">
    <math display="block"><mrow><mi class="cg">w</mi><mo>←</mo><mi class="cg">w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi class="ct">η</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mo class="co">∇</mo><mi class="co">w</mi></msub><mi class="co">L</mi></mrow></math>
    <figcaption class="legend"><span><i class="bg-g"></i><b>w</b> master weight, kept in float32</span><span><i class="bg-t"></i><b>η</b> learning rate</span><span><i class="bg-o"></i><b>∇L</b> gradient, from the bfloat16 maths</span></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Benefits and trade-offs</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="tile t-sage"><h4>Benefits</h4><ul>
      <li>Faster maths: an H100 does about 990 TFLOPS in bfloat16, against 67 in float32. Real training often runs 2 to 3 times faster.</li>
      <li>Activations take half the memory, so bigger batches fit.</li>
      <li>Little or no loss in accuracy.</li>
    </ul></div>
    <div class="tile t-peach"><h4>Trade-offs</h4><ul>
      <li>The model state doesn't shrink: weights, gradients and Adam still need about 16 bytes per parameter.</li>
      <li>bfloat16 needs an Ampere or newer GPU (A100, H100, RTX 30 series and up). On a T4, like free Colab, use float16.</li>
      <li>float16 overflows above 65,504 and loses tiny values, so it needs loss scaling.</li>
    </ul></div>
  </div>

  <h3>In practice</h3>
  <CodeTabs title="Run it yourself" tabs={mixedPrecisionCode} />
  <p><b>Libraries:</b> PyTorch AMP (<code>torch.autocast</code> and <code>torch.amp.GradScaler</code>), Hugging Face Trainer and Accelerate, PyTorch Lightning, Keras (mixed precision policies), and NVIDIA Transformer Engine for 8-bit (FP8) training on H100 and newer GPUs.</p>
  <div class="take">Compute in 16-bit, keep the weights and the update in float32. Training gets faster and activations take half the memory.</div>

  <!-- ================= GRADIENT ACCUMULATION ================= -->
  <h2>Gradient accumulation: several trips</h2>
  <figure class="card" style="background:var(--stage)"><img src={url('assets/img/trips.webp')} alt="One full backpack becomes four smaller backpack trips" style="margin:auto;max-height:120px"></figure>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>Why?</b> Bigger batches give smoother, more stable training. But activation memory grows with the batch size, so the batch you want may not fit on your GPU.</p>

  <h3>How it works</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><span>Split the batch you want into micro-batches that fit: a batch of 64 becomes 4 micro-batches of 16.</span></li>
    <li><span>Run the forward and backward pass on each micro-batch. PyTorch adds the gradients up in <code>.grad</code>. Divide each loss by 4, so the sum becomes the average.</span></li>
    <li><span>After the 4th micro-batch, update the weights once and reset the gradients to zero.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <figure class="eq" aria-label="Effective batch size equals micro-batch size times accumulation steps times number of GPUs">
    <math display="block"><mrow><mtext>Effective batch</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext class="cg">micro-batch</mtext><mo>×</mo><mtext class="ct">accumulation steps</mtext><mo>×</mo><mtext class="cb">GPUs</mtext></mrow></math>
    <figcaption class="legend"><span><i class="bg-g"></i>what fits in memory</span><span><i class="bg-t"></i>micro-batches per update</span><span><i class="bg-b"></i>data-parallel copies (Module 5)</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <Widget title="Batch of 64 on a GPU that fits 16" tag="Step through it" id="w-ga">
    <div class="grid4" id="gaSteps"></div>
    <div style="margin:16px 0 6px;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted)">Accumulated gradient</div>
    <div class="stack" style="height:26px"><span id="gaBar" style="width:0;background:var(--lav)"></span></div>
    <div class="row" style="margin-top:14px"><button class="btn" id="gaNext">Run micro-batch 1</button><button class="btn ghost" id="gaReset">Reset</button></div>
    <div class="verdict mid" id="gaV">Each micro-batch computes gradients but doesn't update the weights yet.</div>
  </Widget>

  <h3>Benefits and trade-offs</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="tile t-sage"><h4>Benefits</h4><ul>
      <li>Train with a big effective batch on a small GPU.</li>
      <li>The same gradient as the big batch, up to tiny rounding differences.</li>
      <li>One setting in most libraries.</li>
    </ul></div>
    <div class="tile t-peach"><h4>Trade-offs</h4><ul>
      <li>Slower than a real big batch: the maths is the same, but small micro-batches keep the GPU less busy.</li>
      <li>Only activation memory shrinks. Weights, gradients and optimizer states stay the same size.</li>
      <li>Fewer updates per epoch, so tune the learning rate for the effective batch size.</li>
    </ul></div>
  </div>

  <h3>In practice</h3>
  <CodeTabs title="Run it yourself" tabs={accumulationCode} />
  <p><b>Libraries:</b> plain PyTorch (a few lines), Hugging Face Trainer (<code>gradient_accumulation_steps</code>) and Accelerate (<code>accelerator.accumulate</code>), PyTorch Lightning (<code>accumulate_grad_batches</code>) and DeepSpeed (<code>gradient_accumulation_steps</code>).</p>
  <div class="take">Gradient accumulation gives big-batch stability with small-batch memory. The cost is time: the micro-batches run one after another.</div>

  <!-- ================= COMPARISON ================= -->
  <h2>Comparing the three tricks</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What changes?</th><th>Main benefit</th><th>Main trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Quantization</b></td><td>How numbers are stored</td><td>Less memory, faster inference</td><td>Some quality loss</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Mixed precision</b></td><td>The precision of the maths</td><td>Faster training, less activation memory</td><td>Numerical care: loss scaling and the right GPU</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Gradient accumulation</b></td><td>When the weights are updated</td><td>Big batches on a small GPU</td><td>Slower than a real big batch</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <div class="take">Optimization is a trade-off between memory, speed, quality and cost.</div>
  <Ben img="ben_think" alt="Ben thinking"><p>So there's no single best trick. Instead of asking "which optimization is best?", I should ask: <b>what is my bottleneck</b>, and <b>what am I willing to trade</b> to remove it?</p></Ben>
</ModuleLayout>

<script>
import { $, fmtNum, logScale, seg } from '../../scripts/lib';

/* ---------- quantize eight numbers ---------- */
const X = [0.21, -0.47, 0.08, 0.92, -0.33, 0.65, -0.12, 0.40];
const qzBits = seg('qzBits', qzCalc), qzScale = seg('qzScale', qzCalc);
function qzCalc() {
  const bits = +qzBits(), group = +qzScale(), outlier = $('#qzOut').checked;
  const x = X.slice(); if (outlier) x[5] = 6.5;
  const qmax = 2 ** (bits - 1) - 1; const scales: number[] = [], q: number[] = [], r: number[] = [];
  for (let g = 0; g < 8; g += group) { const s = Math.max(...x.slice(g, g + group).map(Math.abs)) / qmax; scales.push(s); for (let i = g; i < g + group; i++) { q[i] = Math.round(x[i] / s); r[i] = q[i] * s } }
  const err = x.map((v, i) => Math.abs(v - r[i])), avg = err.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / 8;
  const cls = (e: number) => e < 0.02 ? 'g' : e < 0.08 ? 'y' : 'r', sep = (i: number) => group === 4 && i === 4 ? ' sep' : '';
  $('#qzTable').innerHTML = '<tbody>' + (group === 4 ? '<tr class="grp"><td></td><td colspan="4">group 1</td><td colspan="4" class="sep">group 2</td></tr>' : '')
    + `<tr><th scope="row">Original x</th>${x.map((v, i) => `<td class="${sep(i)}">${v.toFixed(2)}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`
    + `<tr><th scope="row">Stored q</th>${q.map((v, i) => `<td class="${sep(i)}">${v + 0}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`
    + `<tr><th scope="row">Recovered s × q</th>${r.map((v, i) => `<td class="${cls(err[i])}${sep(i)}">${(v + 0).toFixed(2)}</td>`).join('')}</tr></tbody>`;
  $('#qzS').textContent = scales.map(s => s < 0.1 ? s.toFixed(4) : s.toFixed(2)).join(' / '); $('#qzR').textContent = `−${qmax} to ${qmax}`; $('#qzE').textContent = avg.toFixed(3);
  const v = $('#qzV');
  if (outlier && bits < 8 && group === 8) { v.className = 'verdict bad'; v.textContent = 'The outlier stretched the scale, so most small numbers rounded to 0. Try one scale per group of 4.' }
  else if (outlier && bits < 8) { v.className = 'verdict mid'; v.textContent = 'Better: only the group with the outlier lost detail. Real 4-bit formats give every small block, such as 64 numbers, its own scale.' }
  else if (bits === 8) { v.className = 'verdict ok'; v.textContent = '8 bits give 255 levels, so the numbers come back almost exactly, in a quarter of float32\'s memory.' }
  else if (bits === 4) { v.className = 'verdict mid'; v.textContent = '4 bits give only 15 levels: small errors, in an eighth of float32\'s memory.' }
  else { v.className = 'verdict bad'; v.textContent = '2 bits give only 3 levels (−1, 0 and 1), so most of the detail is gone.' }
}
$('#qzOut').addEventListener('input', qzCalc); qzCalc();

/* ---------- quantization: same model, four formats ---------- */
function quantCalc() {
  const N = logScale(+$('#qN').value, 1e8, 4e11); const V = +$('#qV').value; $('#qNo').textContent = fmtNum(N);
  const rows: [string, number][] = [['float32', 4], ['bfloat16', 2], ['int8', 1], ['int4', 0.5]]; const max = Math.max(N * 4 / 1e9, V) * 1.05;
  $('#qBars').innerHTML = rows.map(([n, b]) => { const gb = N * b / 1e9; const fit = gb <= V; return `<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:90px minmax(0,1fr) 110px;gap:10px;align-items:center"><b style="font-size:14px">${n}</b><div class="stack" style="height:30px"><span style="width:${gb / max * 100}%;background:${fit ? '#BFE0B1' : '#F2B8B5'}"></span><i class="limit" data-l="" style="left:${V / max * 100}%"></i></div><span class="mono" style="font-size:14px">${gb.toFixed(1)} GB ${fit ? '✓' : '✗'}</span></div>` }).join('');
}
['qN', 'qV'].forEach(i => $('#' + i).addEventListener('input', quantCalc)); quantCalc();

/* ---------- mixed precision: real bfloat16 rounding ---------- */
const f32 = new Float32Array(1), u32 = new Uint32Array(f32.buffer);
function toBf16(v: number) { f32[0] = v; let x = u32[0]; const lsb = (x >> 16) & 1; x = (x + 0x7FFF + lsb) & 0xFFFF0000; u32[0] = x >>> 0; return f32[0] }
function mpCalc() {
  const w = +$('#mpW').value, u = +$('#mpU').value; f32[0] = w + u; const r32 = f32[0]; const r16 = toBf16(toBf16(w) + toBf16(u));
  $('#mp32').textContent = r32.toPrecision(7); $('#mp16').textContent = r16.toPrecision(7); const v = $('#mpV'); const lost = toBf16(w) === r16 && u !== 0;
  if (lost) { v.className = 'verdict bad'; v.textContent = 'In bfloat16 the update vanished: ' + w + ' + ' + u + ' rounds back to ' + r16 + '. Do this for every weight and the model stops learning.' }
  else { v.className = 'verdict ok'; v.textContent = 'The update survived in both formats here. Try a smaller update, like 0.001 on a weight of 1.' }
}
['mpW', 'mpU'].forEach(i => $('#' + i).addEventListener('input', mpCalc)); mpCalc();

/* ---------- gradient accumulation ---------- */
let ga = 0;
function gaPaint() {
  $('#gaSteps').innerHTML = [1, 2, 3, 4].map(i => `<div class="tile ${i <= ga ? 't-blue' : ''}" style="text-align:center"><h4 style="margin:0">Micro-batch ${i}</h4><p>16 examples${i <= ga ? '<br>gradients added' : ''}</p></div>`).join('');
  $('#gaBar').style.width = (ga / 4 * 100) + '%'; $('#gaBar').textContent = ga ? ga * 16 + ' examples' : ''; const b = $('#gaNext'), v = $('#gaV');
  if (ga < 4) { b.textContent = 'Run micro-batch ' + (ga + 1); v.className = 'verdict mid'; v.textContent = ga ? 'Gradients from ' + ga * 16 + ' examples added. Still no update.' : "Each micro-batch computes gradients but doesn't update the weights yet." }
  else if (ga === 4) { b.textContent = 'Update the weights'; v.className = 'verdict mid'; v.textContent = 'All 64 examples processed with only 16 in memory at a time. Now update once.' }
  else { b.textContent = 'Done'; b.disabled = true; v.className = 'verdict ok'; v.textContent = 'Weights updated once, exactly as if you had used a batch of 64. It took 4 forward and backward passes.' }
}
$('#gaNext').addEventListener('click', () => { if (ga < 5) ga++; gaPaint() }); $('#gaReset').addEventListener('click', () => { ga = 0; $('#gaNext').disabled = false; gaPaint() }); gaPaint();
</script>
